    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. one thing i'm running into is that some of the interactive elements, like the small toggle switches and icon buttons, seem to be totally invisible to the screen reader even though they look fine on screen. it's like these little bugs are just hiding in the code and popping up randomly during testing. any idea what causes those kinds of weird, hidden edge cases?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those \"bugs\" are often due to insufficient screen reader labeling of interactive elements. Check that:\n\n- All interactive elements have an aria-label or alt/provider text, even if the text is not visible.\n- Screen readers can navigate to these elements using the 'header' navigation or a focus indicator when focused.\n- You're not relying on overly subtle or responsive styles that can change an element's appearance without changing its accessible states.\n- Unnecessary use or misapplication of CSS exclusions like 'pointer-events', 'pointer-events: none', or having elements hidden behind complex shapes or overlays.\n\nVerifying the behavior of screen readers is crucial for widespread accessibility.",
      "source": "target",
